's worth protecting and what's not? What is natural? Who gets to answer these questions and whose answers matter? How one answers these questions has deep implications for the thoughts and actions that take place in politics, economics, development, public-health, activism, and indigenous-state relations. This course will critically examine environmentalist discourse through the varied voices of anthropologists, ecologists, philosophers, activists, and indigenous peoples. Of particular focus, we will wrestle with how to understand the relationship between nature (or, \"the environment\") and culture (or, \"society\"), and what political consequences result from such conceptualizations", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall (offered alternate years).", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "First-Year Writing Seminar.", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Improve writing skills: structure coherent essays with clear, main ideas.", "Identify and write for a specific audience.", "Define a personal position on a reading or issue.", "Develop convincing arguments.", "Identify and respond to counter-arguments.", "Make claims based on evidence.", "Compile evidence and analyze its implications."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2678, "crseId": 357763, "crseOfferNbr": 4, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "2350", "titleShort": "Arkeo of North American Indian", "titleLong": "Archaeology of North American Indians", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 16288, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:55PM", "timeEnd": "04:10PM", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "kj21", "firstName": "Kurt", "middleName": "Anders", "lastName": "Jordan"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Morrill Hall 107", "bldgDescr": "Morrill Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "MRL107", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "2350", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ANTHR", "catalogNbr": "2235", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ARKEO", "catalogNbr": "2235", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2017", "sessionEndDt": "12/01/2017", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This introductory course surveys archaeology's contributions to the study of American Indian cultural diversity and change in North America north of Mexico. Lectures and readings will examine topics ranging from the debate over when the continent was first inhabited to present-day conflicts between Native Americans and archaeologists over excavation and the interpretation of the past. We will review important archaeological sites such as Chaco Canyon, Cahokia, Lamoka Lake, and the Little Bighorn battlefield. A principal focus will be on major transformations in lifeways such as the adoption of agriculture, the development of political-economic hierarchies, and the disruptions that accompanied the arrival of Europeans to the continent.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AG, D-AG, HA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2678, "crseId": 352722, "crseOfferNbr": 3, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "2600", "titleShort": "Intro To Native American Lits", "titleLong": "Introduction to Native American Literature", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 4891, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:55PM", "timeEnd": "04:10PM", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "cew254", "firstName": "Carol", "middleName": "Edelman", "lastName": "Warrior"}], "pattern": "MW", "facilityDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall 350", "bldgDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "GSH350", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "2600", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "2600", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2017", "sessionEndDt": "12/01/2017", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "The production of North American Indigenous literatures began long before European colonization, and persists in a variety of printed, sung, carved, painted, written, spoken, and digital media. From oral traditions transmitted through memory and mnemonics to contemporary genres and media, Native North American authors offer Indigenous perspectives on social, political, and environmental experience, through deft artistry and place-specific aesthetics. Our attention will focus on the contexts from which particular Native American literatures emerge, the ethics to consider when entering Indigenous intellectual territory, and close attention to common themes and techniques that frequently appear in contemporary Native American literature. Readings will feature a range of novels, poetry, short fiction, graphic novel/comics, and film.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AG, D-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2678, "crseId": 365706, "crseOfferNbr": 3, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "3560", "titleShort": "Indigenous Philosophies", "titleLong": "Thinking from a Different Place: Indigenous Philosophies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 4930, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "etc7", "firstName": "Eric", "middleName": "T.", "lastName": "Cheyfitz"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Uris Hall 494", "bldgDescr": "Uris Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "URH494", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "3562", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "3560", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2017", "sessionEndDt": "12/01/2017", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "The Western nation-state has failed to solve the two most pressing, indeed catastrophic, global problems: poverty and climate change. This failure is due to the inability of national policy to imagine a world beyond a boundary drawn by the formative capitalist ideas of property, production, and profit. The course will begin by discussing the historical origin and continuing force of these ideas while raising questions about their limits. Then it will look at a range of alternative ideas about how the world should work if we want to keep it socially, economically, and ecologically in balance. The alternatives we will query come from a range of Indigenous writers of fiction, poetry, and theory, who locate themselves in Native American (north and south), Aboriginal, and Maori communities.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2678, "crseId": 366149, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "4625", "titleShort": "Contemp Native Amer Fiction", "titleLong": "Contemporary Native American Fiction", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 16423, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "02:40PM", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "etc7", "firstName": "Eric", "middleName": "T.", "lastName": "Cheyfitz"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Morrill Hall 404", "bldgDescr": "Morrill Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "MRL404", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2017", "endDt": "12/01/2017", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum"